---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';import '../../../../styles/axon-interface.css';
const questions=[
 ['Does an illuminated engineered soma drive a propagating axonal output?','The coupled membrane solves answer this for the declared hypothetical morphology, local light field and expression density. Inspect distal crossings and all matched controls. This does not establish human ganglion physiology.','mathematical'],
 ['Is the active soma necessary?','Compare soma-only removal with regional removal. A strong enough photocurrent can drive the stems even with native somatic channels removed; these are different causal claims.','mathematical'],
 ['Is optical isolation proven when no photons reach a neighbor?','No. The neighbor retains a positive confidence upper bound. Three nonlinear exposure probes are sensitivity tests and cannot prove every interior outcome.','mathematical'],
 ['Does the wall confine magnetic stimulation?','The nonmagnetic wall does not shield primary B. The tissue conductivity boundary changes secondary E. Coil winding and orientation must be tested separately.','mathematical'],
 ['Can a weakly coupled coil still count as successful if current is increased?','Only a computed propagating response can count, and the reported current, resistance and aggregate power remain part of the result. No threshold is extrapolated beyond the sampled grid.','mathematical'],
 ['Can four independent channels be inferred from one selective row?','No. Every declared isolated channel is solved against the entire cohort. Untested channels remain untested.','mathematical'],
 ['Does high matrix rank mean independent simultaneous control?','No. The exact rank describes the sampled binary table. Nonlinear simultaneous inputs and noise require different experiments.','mathematical'],
 ['Can spectral multiplexing multiply the counted outputs?','Only if the full calculated matrix and matched controls support additional distinct outputs. Every wavelength drives every cell through its actual spectral overlap, photon transport and four-state kinetics. In the two-source fixture, blue light also recruits a Chrimson cell; three nonzero input rows yield only two distinct outputs. No channel multiplier is assumed.','mathematical'],
 ['Are all four families experimentally calibrated for these engineered cells?','No. ChR2, Chrimson and vf-Chrimson use explicitly attributed published model parameters; the fourth green construct is hypothetical. Room-temperature kinetics, expression and nerve optical properties are not fitted to this 37 °C engineered preparation. Each run retains rate, expression, normalization and exposure sensitivity probes.','hypothetical'],
 ['Why is original-host addressability zero when an engineered cell fires?','The imposed complete-resection B3 anatomy removes host continuity. The introduced E cell owns its own projections and never becomes an original H identity.','hypothetical'],
 ['What should Stage 3 transfer into the electrical experiment?','Only explicit accepted counts and identities from separate days-clock fate, survival and integration states. Growth must not replace geometry or advance membrane state silently.','estimated'],
 ['Which experiments would most change confidence in the model?','Measured engineered-cell morphologies and channel fits; membrane-scale light maps; validated magnetic field measurements; and causal tests of propagation with matched biological interventions. These are proposed measurements, not existing evidence.','estimated']
];
---
<Layout pageTitle="Modalities & Selectivity · Scientific Questions" description="Questions, calculated answers and unresolved assumptions for the axon-resolution modality experiment." wide compactHeader><article class="ax-lab ax-methods"><span class="ax-kicker">STAGE 2 / SCIENTIFIC QUESTIONS</span><h1>WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS RESULT?</h1><p><a href="../">Laboratory</a> · <a href="../methods/">Methods, numerical evidence and failures</a></p>{questions.map(([q,a,e],i)=><section><h2>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} · {q}</h2><p>{a}</p><small>Evidence class: {e}</small></section>)}</article></Layout>
